A||N||4||Vlad||Replaced Battery||||3||
Z||000000||Vlad||01-27-2001||04:58 AM||||Hello <BR>Due to completely rusted tray, I had to replace it. After considering all three possibilities, I decided to move the battery into the trunk. I got plastic tray off Pontiac Firefly from local junkyard (10$ with some wires). The new tray fits very nicely on the plate next to the blower motor. <BR>However, here are some questions. <BR>I used old cables with added extensions. To connect two pieces together I used small round clamp and some tape for isolation. For now it holds very well, but would it be absolutely necessary to weld those cables..?? <BR>Also, there is a thin red wire from positive terminal. I connected it to the same clamp that holds main positive cable. Originally, it was running from the battery terminal itself. Any problem with this configuration...??? <BR>And the last question. What do I do with that space left after battery removal. Of course, I get rid of what's left of the old tray, but all bottom of that place it very rusted. Can I cover it with anything? Or just paint it? Ideas? <BR>Thank you <BR>85 GT <BR>||||reg||3
Z||000001||rogergarrison||01-27-2001||08:22 AM||||Id recommend that you get new cable to run all the way from battery post to starter. Another connection that unnecessary is a possible problem later on, using starter pulls lots of current, makes a lot of heat. Ground (-) can be anyplace on engine block or head. The little wire on (+) usually has a fusable link going to fusebox. Id just clean up the old area and paint it. Engine compt needs all the cool air you can get. It gives you a ez place to lay parts and tools when your working on it, lol.||||reg||1
Z||000002||RossT||01-27-2001||09:03 AM||||I replaced my battery with an optima to help prevent the famous rusted tray.  They are expensive ($120), but well worth not going thru the rust fix.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005058.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005058.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||LarryB||01-27-2001||09:13 AM||||Ditto on the power cables. Splicing primary power is a *bad* idea. (only time I've seen splicing in is with H-taps, but those are to double up cables for 100+ foot runs of DC power.)<P>For the rust in the old area brush the loose rust off, wash the area to get any battery-acid drippings off, let it dry, then apply some Naval Jelly or other rust neutralizer following the directions.  After it dries use a good quality spray paint.<P>You just want to brush it and get the loose crud off. The idea is to have only solid metal there, not get rid of all the rust. That way the Naval Jelly can properly bond and seal the metal.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-27-2001||01:02 PM||||Leave the area the old tray is in empty. remove the plastic heat sheild if it is still there. That should help open up air flow out the grate.<P>you need to get rid of the rust or treat it with something before painting.<P>If the splice is tight then it should hold for awhile. You need something better than regular electrical tape over it. Get some of the self vulcanizing tape and cover everything with that and then put a good layer of regular tape over it. The vulcanizing tape will seal out water and air. the plastic will protect the joint from abrasion.||||reg||1
